window.MCQ_BANK={"systems":["Respiratory System","Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","Neurological System","Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","Toxicology","Trauma, Burns, Obstetric and Environmental Emergencies","Critical Care Pharmacology and Sedation","Statistics, Ethics and Quality"],"items":[{"n":1,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A 58-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is intubated. The chest radiograph shows bilateral opacities not fully explained by cardiac failure. On PEEP 10 cmH₂O and FiO₂ 0.8, arterial PaO₂ is 104 mmHg. According to the Berlin definition, which category of ARDS applies?","options":["Mild ARDS","Severe ARDS","Acute lung injury (ALI) without ARDS","Moderate ARDS","Not ARDS, because PEEP exceeds 5 cmH₂O"],"answer":"D","explain":"PaO₂/FiO₂ = 104/0.8 = 130 mmHg. Berlin categories (with PEEP ≥5 cmH₂O): mild 200–300, moderate 100–200, severe ≤100 mmHg. The term ALI was removed in the Berlin definition."},{"n":2,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"Which intervention has the strongest randomised evidence for improving survival in severe ARDS (PaO₂/FiO₂ \u003c150 mmHg on FiO₂ ≥0.6 and PEEP ≥5 cmH₂O)?","options":["Inhaled nitric oxide","High-frequency oscillatory ventilation","Maximal lung recruitment with high-PEEP titration","Prone positioning for at least 16 hours per day","Routine early neuromuscular blockade with deep sedation"],"answer":"D","explain":"PROSEVA showed 28-day mortality of 16.0% with proning versus 32.8% supine. Inhaled nitric oxide improves oxygenation but not survival; HFOV (OSCILLATE) and aggressive recruitment (ART) increased mortality; ROSE showed no benefit from routine early neuromuscular blockade."},{"n":3,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A 175 cm tall male patient weighing 110 kg has ARDS. Using predicted body weight (male: 50 + 0.91 × [height in cm − 152.4]), which tidal volume corresponds to 6 mL/kg?","options":["350 mL","420 mL","530 mL","660 mL","770 mL"],"answer":"B","explain":"PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (175 − 152.4) = 70.6 kg; 6 × 70.6 ≈ 424 mL. Lung size relates to height and sex, not actual weight; using 110 kg would give 660 mL, a harmful over-distending volume."},{"n":4,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A ventilated ARDS patient (volume control) has VT 450 mL, plateau pressure 30 cmH₂O and total PEEP 12 cmH₂O. What are the driving pressure and static compliance?","options":["Driving pressure 12 cmH₂O; compliance 37.5 mL/cmH₂O","Driving pressure 18 cmH₂O; compliance 25 mL/cmH₂O","Driving pressure 30 cmH₂O; compliance 15 mL/cmH₂O","Driving pressure 18 cmH₂O; compliance 15 mL/cmH₂O","Driving pressure 42 cmH₂O; compliance 10.7 mL/cmH₂O"],"answer":"B","explain":"Driving pressure = Pplat − PEEP = 30 − 12 = 18 cmH₂O. Static compliance = VT/ΔP = 450/18 = 25 mL/cmH₂O. In pooled ARDS data (Amato), driving pressure was the ventilatory variable most strongly associated with survival; aim for \u003c15 cmH₂O."},{"n":5,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A 66-year-old with a COPD exacerbation, ventilated at RR 24 with I:E 1:1.5, becomes acutely hypotensive (65/40 mmHg) with rising peak pressures. The expiratory flow waveform does not return to zero before the next breath. What is the most appropriate immediate action?","options":["Give a 1 L crystalloid bolus and continue current settings","Increase PEEP to 15 cmH₂O","Increase tidal volume to 10 mL/kg","Disconnect from the ventilator to allow passive exhalation, then reduce rate and lengthen expiratory time","Start norepinephrine and make no ventilator changes"],"answer":"D","explain":"This is dynamic hyperinflation with intrinsic PEEP, reducing venous return and causing hypotension or arrest. Brief disconnection (± chest compression) relieves trapped gas. Then reduce rate and VT, shorten inspiratory time, give bronchodilators and treat the cause. Fluids or vasopressors alone do not address the mechanism."},{"n":6,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A 70-year-old with an acute COPD exacerbation has RR 30, pH 7.27, PaCO₂ 9.2 kPa (69 mmHg) and GCS 15 after optimal medical therapy and controlled oxygen. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":["Immediate endotracheal intubation","High-flow oxygen 15 L/min via non-rebreather mask","Non-invasive ventilation (bilevel positive airway pressure)","IV doxapram as first-line therapy","CPAP alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"NIV is indicated for persistent respiratory acidosis (pH \u003c7.35, PaCO₂ \u003e6.5 kPa) after initial therapy. It reduces intubation, mortality and length of stay. High FiO₂ worsens hypercapnia (target SpO₂ 88–92%); CPAP does not provide ventilatory support; doxapram is inferior to NIV."},{"n":7,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"Which physiological effect best explains the benefit of high-flow nasal oxygen (HFNO) in acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure?","options":["Delivery of PEEP of 15–20 cmH₂O","Reduction of true anatomical shunt","Reduced carbon dioxide production","Direct bronchodilation from humidified gas","Low-level positive airway pressure with washout of nasopharyngeal dead space"],"answer":"E","explain":"At 30–60 L/min HFNO generates modest distending pressure (about 3–5 cmH₂O with the mouth closed), flushes upper-airway dead space, matches patient inspiratory flow (stable FiO₂) and improves comfort and mucociliary clearance. In FLORALI, HFNO reduced 90-day mortality versus NIV or standard oxygen."},{"n":8,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"Which of the following shifts the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT?","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Carboxyhaemoglobin","Hyperthermia","Raised 2,3-DPG","Hypercapnia"],"answer":"B","explain":"Left shift (increased affinity, lower P50) occurs with alkalosis, hypothermia, low 2,3-DPG (stored blood), fetal haemoglobin, carbon monoxide and methaemoglobin. Acidosis, hypercapnia, fever and raised 2,3-DPG shift the curve right, favouring oxygen unloading."},{"n":9,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A patient breathing room air (FiO₂ 0.21; barometric pressure 760 mmHg; water vapour 47 mmHg; R = 0.8) has PaO₂ 55 mmHg and PaCO₂ 48 mmHg. What is the approximate alveolar–arterial oxygen gradient?","options":["10 mmHg","20 mmHg","35 mmHg","60 mmHg","90 mmHg"],"answer":"C","explain":"PAO₂ = 0.21 × (760 − 47) − 48/0.8 = 149.7 − 60 ≈ 90 mmHg. A–a gradient = 90 − 55 ≈ 35 mmHg (normal roughly age/4 + 4). A raised gradient indicates V/Q mismatch, shunt or diffusion limitation in addition to hypoventilation."},{"n":10,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A 72-year-old with COPD and heart failure, ventilated for 6 days, passes a spontaneous breathing trial. Which strategy immediately after extubation best reduces the risk of reintubation?","options":["Standard low-flow oxygen","Prophylactic non-invasive ventilation","Prophylactic IV dexamethasone","Delay extubation for a further 24 hours","Immediate elective tracheostomy"],"answer":"B","explain":"Age \u003e65, chronic cardiac or respiratory disease and hypercapnia identify high risk of extubation failure; prophylactic NIV reduces reintubation and ICU mortality (ERS/ATS guidance). HFNO alone suits low-risk patients; in high-risk patients NIV plus HFNO beat HFNO alone (HIGH-WEAN). Steroids are reserved for patients at risk of post-extubation stridor."},{"n":11,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"Which measure has supporting evidence for reducing the incidence of ventilator-associated pneumonia?","options":["Routine ventilator circuit changes every 48 hours","Stress ulcer prophylaxis with a PPI in all patients","Early tracheostomy performed specifically to reduce VAP","Prophylactic broad-spectrum IV antibiotics in all ventilated patients","Endotracheal tube with subglottic secretion drainage"],"answer":"E","explain":"Subglottic secretion drainage reduces early-onset VAP in meta-analyses. Routine circuit changes give no benefit; acid suppression may increase risk; early tracheostomy does not reduce VAP (TracMan); routine antibiotic prophylaxis is not recommended. Other measures: head-of-bed elevation 30–45°, daily sedation interruption and weaning protocols."},{"n":12,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"A 28-year-old with acute severe asthma has not responded to continuous nebulised salbutamol, ipratropium and IV hydrocortisone. She is exhausted with a silent chest and PaCO₂ 6.5 kPa. Which additional drug is the best next step while preparing for intubation?","options":["IV aminophylline 5 mg/kg bolus","IV amoxicillin–clavulanate","Nebulised N-acetylcysteine","Nebulised hypertonic saline","IV magnesium sulfate 2 g over 20 minutes"],"answer":"E","explain":"A normal or raised PaCO₂ in severe asthma is ominous. A single dose of IV magnesium (1.2–2 g) is recommended for acute severe or life-threatening asthma. Aminophylline is for senior-directed use only; antibiotics are not routine. If intubating, use ketamine, a low rate, long expiratory time and permissive hypercapnia."},{"n":13,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"According to the EOLIA trial entry criteria, which scenario best qualifies a patient with severe ARDS for consideration of VV-ECMO?","options":["PaO₂/FiO₂ 120 mmHg for 2 hours","pH 7.31 with PaCO₂ 52 mmHg","PaO₂/FiO₂ \u003c80 mmHg for more than 6 hours despite FiO₂ ≥0.8, PEEP ≥10 cmH₂O, VT 6 mL/kg and optimisation including proning","PaO₂/FiO₂ 150 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.6","Refractory hypoxaemia with irreversible multiorgan failure and terminal malignancy"],"answer":"C","explain":"EOLIA criteria: PaO₂/FiO₂ \u003c50 for \u003e3 h, or \u003c80 for \u003e6 h, or pH \u003c7.25 with PaCO₂ ≥60 mmHg for \u003e6 h (all despite lung-protective ventilation). ECMO is a rescue therapy for potentially reversible lung injury; irreversible multiorgan failure or terminal illness are contraindications."},{"n":14,"system":"Respiratory System","stem":"What did large randomised trials (e.g. TracMan) conclude about early (≤4 days) versus late tracheostomy in ventilated ICU patients?","options":["No difference in mortality; routine early tracheostomy is not recommended","Early tracheostomy reduced 30-day mortality by about 10%","Early tracheostomy eliminated ventilator-associated pneumonia","Late tracheostomy markedly increased delirium","Early tracheostomy increased long-term mortality"],"answer":"A","explain":"TracMan found 30-day mortality of 30.8% (early) versus 32.5% (late), with no difference in ICU stay or VAP, and many late-group patients never needed a tracheostomy. Timing should be individualised (anticipated prolonged ventilation, comfort, airway and weaning considerations)."},{"n":15,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A 30-year-old with severe diarrhoea has BP 70/40 mmHg and HR 130. Pulmonary artery catheter: CI 1.6 L/min/m², CVP 2 mmHg, PAOP 4 mmHg, SVR raised. Which type of shock is this?","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Distributive (septic) shock","Obstructive shock (tamponade)","Neurogenic shock","Hypovolaemic shock"],"answer":"E","explain":"Low cardiac output with low filling pressures and high SVR indicates hypovolaemia. Cardiogenic: low CO with high PAOP. Distributive: high CO and low SVR. Obstructive: high CVP with low or normal PAOP. Neurogenic: low SVR with bradycardia."},{"n":16,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A 62-year-old with anterior STEMI is in cardiogenic shock. Angiography shows an occluded LAD plus severe RCA and circumflex stenoses. Which revascularisation strategy is supported by the CULPRIT-SHOCK trial?","options":["Culprit-lesion-only PCI, with staged revascularisation of other lesions if needed","Immediate multivessel PCI","Emergency CABG for all patients","Routine intra-aortic balloon pump insertion","Fibrinolysis alone"],"answer":"A","explain":"CULPRIT-SHOCK showed lower 30-day death or renal replacement therapy with culprit-only PCI (45.9% vs 55.4%). IABP-SHOCK II found no mortality benefit from routine IABP, and ECLS-SHOCK showed no survival benefit from routine VA-ECMO."},{"n":17,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A patient in septic shock has received 30 mL/kg of crystalloid and is on norepinephrine 0.35 µg/kg/min with MAP 58 mmHg and lactate 4.5 mmol/L. What is the best next step according to the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021?","options":["Replace norepinephrine with dopamine","Add dobutamine routinely","Give hydroxyethyl starch","Add vasopressin (up to 0.03 U/min)","Give a further 30 mL/kg crystalloid regardless of dynamic assessment"],"answer":"D","explain":"SSC suggests adding vasopressin rather than escalating norepinephrine, typically at 0.25–0.5 µg/kg/min; epinephrine is the next-line addition. Dopamine causes more arrhythmias, starches are harmful, and further fluids should be guided by dynamic indices of responsiveness."},{"n":18,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"In the SMART trial of critically ill adults, balanced crystalloids compared with 0.9% saline were associated with a lower rate of which outcome?","options":["In-hospital mortality (statistically significant)","Need for mechanical ventilation","Major adverse kidney events within 30 days (death, new RRT or persistent renal dysfunction)","Hyperkalaemia","ICU length of stay"],"answer":"C","explain":"SMART: MAKE30 was 14.3% with balanced crystalloids versus 15.4% with saline, driven by death, new RRT and persistent dysfunction. In-hospital mortality was not significantly different. The later PLUS trial found no 90-day mortality difference between Plasma-Lyte and saline."},{"n":19,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A 68-year-old two days after CABG develops AF at 150/min with BP 78/45 mmHg, cool peripheries and rising lactate. What is the most appropriate management?","options":["IV diltiazem 20 mg","IV metoprolol","IV digoxin loading","Synchronised DC cardioversion","IV amiodarone infusion alone"],"answer":"D","explain":"Tachyarrhythmia with haemodynamic compromise needs synchronised cardioversion (e.g. 120–150 J biphasic). Amiodarone may be used as an adjunct. Diltiazem and beta-blockers are negatively inotropic and unsafe in hypotension. Correct potassium (\u003e4 mmol/L) and magnesium."},{"n":20,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"What was the principal finding of the TTM2 trial in comatose survivors of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest?","options":["Hypothermia at 33 °C improved 6-month survival","Targeted hypothermia improved neurological outcome only in non-shockable rhythms","Early hyperoxia improved survival","Fever control was harmful","Hypothermia at 33 °C did not improve 6-month survival compared with normothermia with early fever treatment"],"answer":"E","explain":"TTM2 reported 6-month mortality of 50% (hypothermia) versus 48% (normothermia), with more arrhythmia in the hypothermia group. Current practice emphasises active prevention of fever (≤37.5 °C) for at least 72 hours, with avoidance of hypoxia, hyperoxia and hypotension."},{"n":21,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"During cardiac arrest with persistent ventricular fibrillation, which drugs should be given after the third shock according to ERC guidelines?","options":["Atropine 3 mg","Calcium chloride and sodium bicarbonate","Adrenaline 1 mg and amiodarone 300 mg","Lidocaine 100 mg and magnesium 2 g","Adrenaline 10 mg"],"answer":"C","explain":"In shockable rhythms give adrenaline 1 mg and amiodarone 300 mg after the third shock; adrenaline is then repeated every 3–5 minutes, with amiodarone 150 mg after the fifth shock. Lidocaine is an alternative if amiodarone is unavailable; atropine is no longer recommended; magnesium is for torsades."},{"n":22,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A 45-year-old, 8 days post hip arthroplasty, collapses with SBP 70 mmHg unresponsive to fluids, tachycardia and a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle on echocardiography. What is the most appropriate therapy?","options":["LMWH alone","Systemic thrombolysis (e.g. alteplase 100 mg over 2 hours) with concurrent unfractionated heparin","Inferior vena cava filter alone","Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 3 L crystalloid","Oral rivaroxaban loading"],"answer":"B","explain":"Haemodynamically unstable (high-risk) PE requires prompt reperfusion; systemic thrombolysis is first line unless contraindicated, with surgical embolectomy or catheter-directed therapy as alternatives. Excess fluid overdistends the RV; use norepinephrine for support."},{"n":23,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A 55-year-old has an acute uncomplicated type B aortic dissection with HR 110 and BP 190/100 mmHg. What is the correct initial pharmacological approach?","options":["IV sodium nitroprusside alone","IV hydralazine alone","IV GTN alone","Immediate IV nifedipine","IV esmolol to heart rate \u003c60/min, adding a vasodilator if needed to reach SBP 100–120 mmHg"],"answer":"E","explain":"The goal is to reduce aortic wall stress (dP/dt). A beta-blocker is given first because a vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia and increases dP/dt. Targets: HR ≈60/min, SBP 100–120 mmHg. Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers are used if beta-blockers are contraindicated."},{"n":24,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"Which echocardiographic finding is most characteristic of cardiac tamponade?","options":["Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve","Diastolic collapse of the right atrium and right ventricle with a plethoric IVC","Regional wall-motion abnormality","Systolic septal flattening with McConnell's sign","E/e′ ratio \u003e15"],"answer":"B","explain":"Chamber collapse occurs when intrapericardial pressure exceeds intracavitary pressure (RA in late diastole, RV in early diastole). Other features: plethoric IVC and exaggerated respiratory variation in transvalvular flow (pulsus paradoxus \u003e10 mmHg). Treat by pericardiocentesis or surgical drainage."},{"n":25,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"What is the mechanism of action of levosimendan?","options":["Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition","β₁-adrenergic receptor agonism","Calcium sensitisation of troponin C and opening of ATP-sensitive potassium channels","Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition","Vasopressin V₁ receptor agonism"],"answer":"C","explain":"Levosimendan increases contractility without raising intracellular calcium or myocardial oxygen consumption, and vasodilates via K-ATP channel opening. An active metabolite prolongs the effect for days. Hypotension is the main adverse effect; trials have not shown a clear mortality benefit."},{"n":26,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"Which statement about the passive leg raising (PLR) test is correct?","options":["Response is judged by the rise in blood pressure alone","It is invalid during spontaneous breathing","It requires a tidal volume ≥8 mL/kg","A rise in stroke volume or cardiac output of ≥10% predicts fluid responsiveness, and the test is valid in spontaneous breathing and arrhythmia","It begins from the supine position"],"answer":"D","explain":"PLR starts semi-recumbent (45°) and then raises the legs with the trunk supine, shifting about 300 mL of blood centrally. It must be assessed with a real-time measure of flow (e.g. LVOT VTI, pulse contour), since BP can mislead. It is unreliable with intra-abdominal hypertension or leg compression."},{"n":27,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"Transthoracic echo: LVOT diameter 2.0 cm, LVOT VTI 20 cm, heart rate 80/min. What is the cardiac output?","options":["2.5 L/min","3.8 L/min","5.0 L/min","6.3 L/min","8.0 L/min"],"answer":"C","explain":"LVOT area = π × (1.0 cm)² = 3.14 cm². Stroke volume = area × VTI = 3.14 × 20 = 62.8 mL. Cardiac output = 62.8 × 80 ≈ 5.0 L/min."},{"n":28,"system":"Cardiovascular System and Resuscitation","stem":"A haemodynamically stable patient on haloperidol and ondansetron has recurrent torsades de pointes with QTc 560 ms. What is first-line treatment?","options":["IV amiodarone 300 mg","IV procainamide","IV sotalol","IV flecainide","IV magnesium sulfate 2 g over 10–15 minutes and withdrawal of QT-prolonging drugs"],"answer":"E","explain":"Magnesium suppresses early afterdepolarisations and is given irrespective of serum level; correct potassium to \u003e4.5 mmol/L. Overdrive pacing or isoprenaline is used for bradycardia-dependent torsades; defibrillate if unstable. Amiodarone, procainamide, sotalol and flecainide prolong QT or are pro-arrhythmic here."},{"n":29,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"A patient with severe TBI has MAP 80 mmHg and ICP 25 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure, and what target range does the Brain Trauma Foundation (4th edition) recommend?","options":["CPP 105 mmHg; target 80–100 mmHg","CPP 55 mmHg; target \u003e80 mmHg","CPP 45 mmHg; target 50–60 mmHg","CPP 65 mmHg; target 40–50 mmHg","CPP 55 mmHg; target 60–70 mmHg"],"answer":"E","explain":"CPP = MAP − ICP = 80 − 25 = 55 mmHg. The recommended target is 60–70 mmHg; pushing CPP above 70 mmHg risks ARDS without clear benefit. Treat ICP above 22 mmHg."},{"n":30,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"A patient has convulsive status epilepticus persisting after adequate IV lorazepam. What did the ESETT trial find regarding second-line agents?","options":["Fosphenytoin was clearly superior","Levetiracetam was clearly superior","Valproate was superior but hepatotoxic","Levetiracetam, fosphenytoin and valproate had similar efficacy (about 45–47% seizure cessation)","None of the agents was better than placebo"],"answer":"D","explain":"ESETT showed no significant difference between the three drugs, so selection depends on context: valproate should be avoided in women of childbearing potential, phenytoin carries arrhythmia and hypotension risks, and levetiracetam needs renal dose adjustment. Refractory cases need anaesthetic agents with EEG monitoring."},{"n":31,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"A 70-year-old presents 2 hours after onset of left hemiparesis; CT excludes haemorrhage and BP is 210/115 mmHg. What BP is required before IV thrombolysis?","options":["\u003c220/120 mmHg","\u003c185/110 mmHg","\u003c160/90 mmHg","\u003c140/90 mmHg","\u003c130/80 mmHg"],"answer":"B","explain":"BP must be below 185/110 mmHg before thrombolysis, then maintained below 180/105 mmHg for 24 hours (labetalol, urapidil or nicardipine). Thrombolysis is allowed up to 4.5 hours; thrombectomy for large-vessel occlusion extends to 24 hours with imaging selection (DAWN, DEFUSE-3)."},{"n":32,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"Which intervention has been shown to improve neurological outcome after aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage?","options":["Prophylactic hypervolaemic therapy","Prophylactic induced hypertension","Routine prophylactic phenytoin","Statin therapy","Oral nimodipine 60 mg 4-hourly for 21 days"],"answer":"E","explain":"Nimodipine reduces poor outcome after SAH, even though its effect is not mediated by reversing angiographic vasospasm. Triple-H therapy and prophylactic hypervolaemia confer no benefit; aim for euvolaemia. Early aneurysm securing is essential."},{"n":33,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"Which finding is compatible with a diagnosis of brainstem death?","options":["Spontaneous respiratory effort during the apnoea test","A preserved pupillary light reflex","A preserved corneal reflex","Spinal reflex movements (e.g. deep tendon reflexes or a Lazarus sign)","A preserved gag reflex"],"answer":"D","explain":"Spinal reflexes can persist because they are mediated below the brainstem. Absent brainstem reflexes (pupillary, corneal, oculovestibular, gag, cough) and apnoea with adequate PaCO₂ rise are required, after excluding confounders (sedatives, hypothermia, metabolic or endocrine causes). Diabetes insipidus and haemodynamic instability are common."},{"n":34,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"In Guillain–Barré syndrome, which '20/30/40' thresholds predict impending respiratory failure?","options":["FVC \u003c40 mL/kg, MIP weaker than −20 cmH₂O, MEP \u003c30 cmH₂O","FVC \u003c30 mL/kg, MIP weaker than −40 cmH₂O, MEP \u003c20 cmH₂O","FVC \u003c20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than −10 cmH₂O, MEP \u003c60 cmH₂O","FVC \u003c10 mL/kg, MIP weaker than −50 cmH₂O, MEP \u003c40 cmH₂O","FVC \u003c20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than −30 cmH₂O, MEP \u003c40 cmH₂O"],"answer":"E","explain":"Also consider ventilation for a \u003e30% fall in FVC, bulbar weakness or autonomic instability. Treat with IVIG (0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days) or plasma exchange; steroids are ineffective. Avoid succinylcholine because of hyperkalaemia risk."},{"n":35,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"Which intervention has the best evidence for preventing ICU delirium?","options":["Prophylactic haloperidol","Prophylactic quetiapine","Benzodiazepine-based sedation","A multicomponent non-pharmacological bundle (early mobilisation, sleep promotion, reorientation, minimising benzodiazepines)","Routine physical restraints"],"answer":"D","explain":"Prophylactic antipsychotics did not reduce delirium in REDUCE or MIND-USA. Benzodiazepines are an independent risk factor. ABCDEF-bundle-style care is beneficial. Dexmedetomidine may shorten time to extubation in agitated delirium (MIDEX, SPICE III)."},{"n":36,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"In the RESCUEicp trial of decompressive craniectomy for refractory intracranial hypertension (ICP \u003e25 mmHg) after TBI, which result was observed?","options":["Lower mortality but higher rates of vegetative state and severe disability at 6 months","Higher mortality with no change in functional outcome","No difference in mortality or functional outcome","Improved favourable outcome in all patients","Benefit only if surgery was performed before ICP exceeded 20 mmHg"],"answer":"A","explain":"Six-month mortality was 26.9% (surgery) versus 48.9% (medical), but vegetative state was 8.5% versus 2.1% and lower severe disability 21.9% versus 14.4%. In DECRA, early craniectomy for modest ICP elevation produced worse outcomes. Decision-making must include discussion of acceptable outcomes."},{"n":37,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"According to ERC–ESICM guidelines, when should multimodal prognostication after cardiac arrest begin?","options":["At 72 hours or more after ROSC, once normothermia is restored and sedative confounders are excluded","Immediately after ROSC using EEG","At 12 hours using pupillary reflexes alone","At 24 hours using neuron-specific enolase alone","At 48 hours using CT alone"],"answer":"A","explain":"Poor outcome is likely if two or more of the following are present: absent pupillary and corneal reflexes at ≥72 h, bilaterally absent N20 SSEP, highly malignant EEG after 24 h, NSE \u003e60 µg/L at 48 and/or 72 h, status myoclonus within 72 h, or diffuse anoxic injury on CT/MRI. Avoid self-fulfilling prophecy."},{"n":38,"system":"Neurological System","stem":"Which neuromuscular blocker response is characteristic of myasthenia gravis?","options":["Increased sensitivity to succinylcholine and resistance to non-depolarising agents","Normal response to both classes","Resistance to both classes","Exaggerated hyperkalaemia with succinylcholine","Resistance to succinylcholine with increased sensitivity to non-depolarising agents"],"answer":"E","explain":"Reduced acetylcholine receptor numbers make patients sensitive to non-depolarising blockers (use small doses, with sugammadex available) and relatively resistant to succinylcholine. Myasthenic crisis is treated with IVIG or plasma exchange; avoid aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, magnesium and beta-blockers."},{"n":39,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"A patient's baseline creatinine is 80 µmol/L. On ICU admission with septic shock it is 250 µmol/L, and urine output is 0.6 mL/kg/h over 12 hours. What is the KDIGO AKI stage?","options":["Not AKI","Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":"D","explain":"250/80 = 3.1× baseline, and stage 3 is defined as ≥3.0× baseline (or ≥354 µmol/L, or RRT initiation). Stage 1 is 1.5–1.9× or a rise ≥26.5 µmol/L in 48 h; stage 2 is 2.0–2.9×. KDIGO has only three stages."},{"n":40,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"What did the STARRT-AKI trial show about accelerated versus standard initiation of RRT in severe AKI?","options":["Accelerated initiation reduced 90-day mortality","No difference in 90-day mortality; more RRT dependence at 90 days with the accelerated strategy","Accelerated initiation shortened ICU stay","The standard strategy increased mortality","Dialysis dependence was identical in both arms"],"answer":"B","explain":"STARRT-AKI: 90-day mortality 43.9% versus 43.7%, but dialysis dependence was 10.4% versus 6.0% with accelerated initiation. AKIKI and IDEAL-ICU agree. Start RRT early only for refractory hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload or uraemic complications."},{"n":41,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"A patient with K⁺ 7.2 mmol/L has peaked T waves and a widening QRS. Which treatment should be given FIRST?","options":["IV insulin with glucose","Nebulised salbutamol 10–20 mg","Sodium bicarbonate 50 mmol","IV calcium (10% calcium gluconate 30 mL or 10% calcium chloride 10 mL)","Oral calcium resonium"],"answer":"D","explain":"Calcium stabilises the myocardial membrane within minutes (effect lasts 30–60 min). Then shift potassium (insulin 10 U with 25 g glucose, salbutamol), remove it (loop diuretics, binders, RRT) and treat the cause."},{"n":42,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"A 55-year-old on a thiazide is admitted with a generalised seizure; serum Na⁺ is 108 mmol/L. What is the best initial management?","options":["Fluid restriction alone","1 L of 0.9% saline over 1 hour","150 mL of 3% saline over 20 minutes, repeated up to twice to raise sodium by 4–6 mmol/L","Oral tolvaptan","IV furosemide alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Severe symptomatic hyponatraemia needs hypertonic saline boluses. Limit correction to ≤10 mmol/L in 24 hours (≤8 mmol/L if at high risk of osmotic demyelination: alcohol, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, liver disease). If overcorrected, give desmopressin and/or 5% dextrose."},{"n":43,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"A 70 kg man (total body water 0.6 × weight) has serum Na⁺ 160 mmol/L. What is the approximate free-water deficit?","options":["2 L","4 L","6 L","8 L","10 L"],"answer":"C","explain":"Deficit = TBW × (Na⁺/140 − 1) = 42 × (160/140 − 1) = 42 × 0.143 ≈ 6 L. Correct sodium no faster than 10–12 mmol/L per 24 hours to avoid cerebral oedema, using enteral water or hypotonic fluid, and allow for ongoing losses."},{"n":44,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"Arterial blood gas: pH 7.24, PaCO₂ 24 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 10 mmol/L. Na⁺ 140, Cl⁻ 100 mmol/L, albumin 40 g/L. Which interpretation is correct?","options":["High anion gap metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory alkalosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis with concomitant metabolic alkalosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation"],"answer":"E","explain":"Anion gap = 140 − (100 + 10) = 30. Winter's formula: expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × 10 + 8 = 23 ± 2 mmHg, so 24 is appropriate. Delta ratio = (30 − 12)/(24 − 10) ≈ 1.3, consistent with a pure HAGMA (1–2). Think lactate, ketones, uraemia and toxins."},{"n":45,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"Which electrolyte pattern is characteristic of early severe rhabdomyolysis?","options":["Hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia","Hypernatraemia and hypophosphataemia","Hypokalaemia and hypercalcaemia","Hypomagnesaemia and hypokalaemia","Hypophosphataemia and hypermagnesaemia"],"answer":"A","explain":"Damaged muscle releases potassium and phosphate, while calcium deposits in injured muscle. Treat hypocalcaemia only if symptomatic or with life-threatening hyperkalaemia, as rebound hypercalcaemia follows. Early fluid resuscitation is key; routine bicarbonate or mannitol is not proven."},{"n":46,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"During CRRT with regional citrate anticoagulation, which finding most suggests citrate accumulation?","options":["Total-to-ionised calcium ratio \u003e2.5 with worsening metabolic acidosis","Rising ionised calcium with metabolic alkalosis","Falling total calcium with metabolic alkalosis","Hyperphosphataemia with hypernatraemia","Thrombocytopenia with hypokalaemia"],"answer":"A","explain":"Citrate is metabolised by liver and muscle to bicarbonate. In liver failure or shock it accumulates, giving a high total calcium (citrate-bound), low ionised calcium and anion gap acidosis. Reduce the citrate dose, increase calcium replacement or switch anticoagulation."},{"n":47,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"A patient has persistent hypokalaemia (K⁺ 2.8 mmol/L) despite repeated potassium replacement. Which coexisting abnormality should be sought and corrected?","options":["Hypocalcaemia","Hyperphosphataemia","Hypomagnesaemia","Hyponatraemia","Hyperchloraemic acidosis"],"answer":"C","explain":"Magnesium depletion impairs Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and opens renal ROMK channels, causing renal potassium wasting; potassium will not correct until magnesium is replaced. Common causes are loop and thiazide diuretics, PPIs, amphotericin and aminoglycosides."},{"n":48,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"In the SAFE study subgroup analyses, which patient group had higher mortality with 4% albumin compared with saline?","options":["Patients with severe sepsis","Patients with ARDS","Patients with traumatic brain injury","Cardiac surgery patients","Patients with burns"],"answer":"C","explain":"In SAFE-TBI, 24-month mortality was 33.2% with albumin versus 20.4% with saline (RR 1.63), probably from increased intracranial pressure. Albumin is otherwise used in cirrhosis (large-volume paracentesis, SBP, HRS)."},{"n":49,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"Which resuscitation fluid has been associated with an increased need for renal replacement therapy and, in some trials, higher mortality in sepsis?","options":["Lactated Ringer's solution","Plasma-Lyte","4% albumin","Gelatin-free balanced crystalloid","Hydroxyethyl starch"],"answer":"E","explain":"6S and CHEST showed increased RRT requirement with HES; regulators have restricted or suspended its use. Balanced crystalloids are first-line for most critically ill patients."},{"n":50,"system":"Renal, Fluid, Electrolyte and Acid–Base Disorders","stem":"A patient with persistent vomiting has pH 7.55 and HCO₃⁻ 42 mmol/L. Which urinary finding indicates chloride-responsive metabolic alkalosis?","options":["Urine chloride \u003e40 mmol/L","Urine sodium \u003e100 mmol/L","Urine chloride \u003c20 mmol/L","Urine osmolality \u003c100 mOsm/kg","Urine pH \u003e8"],"answer":"C","explain":"Vomiting or nasogastric loss causes volume and chloride depletion, so the kidney retains chloride (low urine Cl⁻). Treat with 0.9% saline and potassium; consider acetazolamide if volume overloaded. Chloride-resistant causes (hyperaldosteronism, Bartter/Gitelman, current diuretics) have urine Cl⁻ \u003e20 mmol/L."},{"n":51,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"A cirrhotic patient with variceal haemorrhage and Hb 82 g/L is being resuscitated. Which transfusion strategy is recommended?","options":["Transfuse to Hb \u003e100 g/L","Restrictive strategy: transfuse at Hb \u003c70 g/L, targeting 70–80 g/L","FFP to normalise the INR","Platelets for all patients","Large-volume crystalloid to restore normal BP"],"answer":"B","explain":"Villanueva et al. showed better survival with a restrictive strategy, as over-transfusion raises portal pressure. Also give a vasoactive drug (terlipressin or octreotide), ceftriaxone prophylaxis, endoscopic band ligation within 12 h, and consider early TIPS in Child–Pugh C (10–13) patients."},{"n":52,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"Which patients are at highest risk of clinically important stress-related GI bleeding and benefit most from stress ulcer prophylaxis?","options":["All ICU patients regardless of risk","Patients receiving enteral nutrition only","Patients with sepsis without coagulopathy","Those with mechanical ventilation \u003e48 hours or coagulopathy","All patients staying \u003e24 hours in ICU"],"answer":"D","explain":"Cook's classic study identified respiratory failure and coagulopathy as independent risk factors. SUP-ICU and REVISE showed pantoprazole reduces bleeding without altering mortality, with possible increases in C. difficile and pneumonia; stop prophylaxis when risk resolves."},{"n":53,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"Which statement regarding the management of severe acute pancreatitis is correct?","options":["Prophylactic carbapenems are recommended to prevent infected necrosis","Aggressive fluid resuscitation is superior to moderate resuscitation","Early enteral feeding (within 24–72 hours) is preferred to parenteral nutrition","Urgent ERCP is indicated in all patients","Patients should remain nil by mouth until pain-free for 2 weeks"],"answer":"C","explain":"Enteral nutrition reduces infection and preserves gut barrier function. WATERFALL found aggressive fluids caused more fluid overload (20.5% vs 6.3%) without benefit, so moderate resuscitation with Ringer's lactate is preferred. Infected necrosis follows a delayed step-up approach (PANTER, TENSION)."},{"n":54,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"Which is a King's College criterion for liver transplantation in paracetamol-induced acute liver failure?","options":["Arterial pH \u003c7.30 after adequate fluid resuscitation","Peak ALT \u003e10,000 U/L","INR \u003e3 alone","Bilirubin \u003e100 µmol/L","Paracetamol level \u003e300 mg/L at admission"],"answer":"A","explain":"King's criteria for paracetamol: arterial pH \u003c7.30 (after fluids) or, together, INR \u003e6.5, creatinine \u003e300 µmol/L and grade 3–4 encephalopathy. Lactate \u003e3 mmol/L after resuscitation has been added. N-acetylcysteine is given regardless of presentation time."},{"n":55,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"In acute liver failure with grade 3–4 encephalopathy, which strategy helps prevent cerebral oedema?","options":["Hypotonic fluid administration","Maintaining serum sodium at 145–155 mmol/L with hypertonic saline","Prophylactic corticosteroids","Sustained hyperventilation to PaCO₂ 20 mmHg","Prophylactic lactulose as the sole measure"],"answer":"B","explain":"Hypertonic saline prevents intracranial hypertension. Avoid hyponatraemia, fever and hyperammonaemia (arterial ammonia \u003e150–200 µmol/L predicts herniation); early CRRT can lower ammonia. Hyperventilation is only a brief rescue; steroids confer no benefit."},{"n":56,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"A patient with decompensated cirrhosis has creatinine rising to 220 µmol/L despite 48 hours of diuretic withdrawal and albumin 1 g/kg, with normal urinary sediment and no proteinuria. What is the treatment?","options":["Low-dose dopamine","Large-volume paracentesis without albumin","A vasoconstrictor (terlipressin or norepinephrine) with albumin","Large-volume 0.9% saline","Furosemide infusion"],"answer":"C","explain":"This is hepatorenal syndrome–AKI. Vasoconstrictors reverse renal vasodilatation-driven hypoperfusion (terlipressin in CONFIRM) and are given with albumin 20–40 g/day. RRT is a bridge to transplantation in suitable patients; avoid NSAIDs and nephrotoxins."},{"n":57,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"What was the principal finding of the EPaNIC trial?","options":["Early parenteral nutrition reduced mortality","Early parenteral nutrition reduced ventilator days","Withholding supplemental parenteral nutrition until day 8 led to fewer infections and faster recovery","Late parenteral nutrition increased infections","No difference in any outcome"],"answer":"C","explain":"Casaer et al. showed fewer ICU infections and shorter ICU stay with late PN. Guidelines recommend early enteral nutrition within 48 hours, with gradual calorie progression; supplemental PN only after day 3–7 if enteral targets are not met. NUTRIREA-2 found no mortality difference between enteral and parenteral routes in shock."},{"n":58,"system":"Gastrointestinal, Hepatic and Nutrition","stem":"Which biochemical abnormality is the hallmark of refeeding syndrome?","options":["Hyperphosphataemia","Hypernatraemia","Hypophosphataemia (with hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia)","Hyperkalaemia","Hypercalcaemia"],"answer":"C","explain":"Carbohydrate feeding releases insulin, shifting phosphate, potassium and magnesium intracellularly. Give thiamine before feeding, start at 10–20 kcal/kg/day, and replace electrolytes. High-risk groups include BMI \u003c16, little intake for \u003e5 days, and alcohol misuse."},{"n":59,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"In the TRISS trial of septic shock, comparing transfusion thresholds of 70 versus 90 g/L, what was found?","options":["The higher threshold reduced 90-day mortality","No difference in 90-day mortality, with fewer transfusions in the lower-threshold group","The lower threshold increased ischaemic events","The lower threshold increased mortality","The higher threshold reduced renal replacement therapy"],"answer":"B","explain":"TRISS: 90-day mortality 43.0% versus 45.0% (not significant). A restrictive threshold of about 70 g/L is appropriate for most ICU patients. Exceptions include acute myocardial infarction, where evidence favours higher thresholds."},{"n":60,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"A 34-year-old has fever, confusion, Hb 70 g/L with schistocytes, platelets 12 × 10⁹/L, creatinine 150 µmol/L, normal PT/aPTT and ADAMTS13 activity \u003c10%. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","options":["Urgent therapeutic plasma exchange with corticosteroids (± caplacizumab and rituximab)","Platelet transfusion to \u003e50 × 10⁹/L","FFP infusion alone, then wait","Heparin infusion","Splenectomy"],"answer":"A","explain":"This is TTP; untreated mortality exceeds 90%. Plasma exchange replaces ADAMTS13 and removes autoantibodies. Platelet transfusion may worsen thrombosis and is reserved for life-threatening bleeding. Normal coagulation tests distinguish TTP from DIC."},{"n":61,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"Which laboratory finding best helps distinguish DIC from the coagulopathy of liver failure?","options":["Prolonged PT","Low factor VIII activity in DIC (normal or high in liver failure)","Low fibrinogen","Thrombocytopenia","Raised D-dimer"],"answer":"B","explain":"All other findings occur in both conditions. Factor VIII is synthesised by endothelium and is therefore preserved or raised in liver failure but consumed in DIC. Manage the underlying cause; transfuse platelets if \u003c10 × 10⁹/L (or \u003c50 with bleeding) and replace fibrinogen if \u003c1.5 g/L with bleeding."},{"n":62,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"Day 7 of unfractionated heparin after cardiac surgery: platelets fall from 320 to 140 × 10⁹/L, and the patient develops a new DVT. What is the next step?","options":["Stop all heparin (including flushes) and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban or bivalirudin","Continue heparin; platelets will recover","Switch to LMWH","Transfuse platelets","Start warfarin immediately"],"answer":"A","explain":"A \u003e50% platelet fall at day 5–10 with thrombosis gives a high 4Ts score and suggests HIT; send a PF4 antibody assay but do not wait for results. LMWH cross-reacts, platelets may fuel thrombosis, and warfarin risks venous limb gangrene until platelets recover."},{"n":63,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"Which massive-transfusion strategy is recommended for exsanguinating trauma?","options":["Red cells only until Hb \u003e100 g/L, then plasma","3 L of crystalloid before any blood products","Fibrinogen concentrate before any red cells","Balanced blood product ratio (about 1:1:1 red cells:plasma:platelets) with early tranexamic acid (1 g within 3 hours of injury)","Platelets only if count \u003c100 × 10⁹/L without bleeding"],"answer":"D","explain":"PROPPR (1:1:1 vs 1:1:2) showed no difference in 24-hour or 30-day mortality but less death from exsanguination at 24 hours with 1:1:1. CRASH-2 showed that TXA 1 g over 10 minutes then 1 g over 8 hours reduces death if started within 3 hours. Also give calcium and maintain fibrinogen \u003e1.5–2 g/L."},{"n":64,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"A patient on dabigatran has a life-threatening intracranial haemorrhage. Which reversal agent is specific?","options":["Andexanet alfa","Idarucizumab 5 g IV","Protamine sulfate","Vitamin K 10 mg IV","Fresh frozen plasma 15 mL/kg"],"answer":"B","explain":"Idarucizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran (RE-VERSE AD); dabigatran is also dialysable. Andexanet alfa targets factor Xa inhibitors (rivaroxaban, apixaban); 4-factor PCC is an alternative when specific agents are unavailable."},{"n":65,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"A patient on warfarin has an INR of 9.0 and an intracranial haemorrhage. What is the best management?","options":["Oral vitamin K 1 mg alone","FFP alone 10 mL/kg","4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (dose by INR) plus vitamin K 5–10 mg IV","Stop warfarin and observe","Recombinant factor VIIa alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"PCC corrects the INR within minutes with a small volume, whereas FFP is slow and risks volume overload. Vitamin K provides sustained correction (6–12 hours to act) and prevents rebound. Recheck the INR after PCC."},{"n":66,"system":"Haematology, Coagulation and Transfusion","stem":"Within 4 hours of plasma transfusion a patient develops hypoxaemia, bilateral infiltrates, fever and hypotension. BNP is normal and there is no response to diuretics. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)","Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)","ABO-incompatible haemolytic reaction","Anaphylaxis","Septic transfusion reaction"],"answer":"B","explain":"TRALI is non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema within 6 hours, caused by donor HLA/HNA antibodies, with hypotension and normal BNP. TACO features hypertension, raised JVP, raised BNP (\u003e1.5× baseline) and diuretic response. Management of TRALI is supportive, with donor investigation."},{"n":67,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"According to Sepsis-3, septic shock is defined as sepsis with:","options":["Systolic BP \u003c90 mmHg after 30 mL/kg crystalloid alone","Two or more SIRS criteria with lactate \u003e4 mmol/L","Vasopressor requirement to maintain MAP ≥65 mmHg and lactate \u003e2 mmol/L despite adequate volume resuscitation","Lactate \u003e4 mmol/L alone","SOFA score increase ≥2 alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Sepsis is life-threatening organ dysfunction (acute SOFA rise ≥2) caused by a dysregulated host response to infection. Septic shock carries hospital mortality above 40%. qSOFA (RR ≥22, altered mentation, SBP ≤100) is a bedside prompt, not a definition."},{"n":68,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"According to the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021, what is the recommended timing of antimicrobials in an adult with possible sepsis but without shock?","options":["Within 3 hours if concern for infection persists after rapid assessment (within 1 hour for shock or high likelihood of sepsis)","Within 1 hour in all cases","Within 6 hours","Only after culture results are available","After 24 hours of observation"],"answer":"A","explain":"SSC 2021 distinguishes shock or probable sepsis (antimicrobials within 1 hour) from possible sepsis without shock (time-limited rapid investigation, then antimicrobials within 3 hours if infection remains likely). Obtain cultures first if it does not delay treatment."},{"n":69,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"Which antibiotic should NOT be used to treat pneumonia because it is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant?","options":["Linezolid","Vancomycin","Daptomycin","Ceftaroline","Teicoplanin"],"answer":"C","explain":"Daptomycin binds surfactant and loses activity in the alveoli. It is effective for bacteraemia, right-sided endocarditis and skin and soft-tissue infection; monitor CK."},{"n":70,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"A critically ill patient in septic shock has candidaemia and previous azole exposure. What is the first-line antifungal?","options":["Fluconazole","An echinocandin (e.g. micafungin or caspofungin)","Voriconazole","Oral nystatin","Flucytosine monotherapy"],"answer":"B","explain":"Echinocandins are first line in critically ill or azole-exposed patients (IDSA). Remove central venous catheters, perform dilated eye examination, and treat for 14 days after the first negative blood culture. De-escalate to fluconazole if the isolate is susceptible and the patient is stable."},{"n":71,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"What is the role of procalcitonin in current Surviving Sepsis recommendations?","options":["To support decisions to discontinue antimicrobials, alongside clinical evaluation","To decide whether to start antibiotics in septic shock","To distinguish bacterial from viral infection definitively","To predict fluid responsiveness","To replace blood cultures"],"answer":"A","explain":"Procalcitonin-guided stopping reduces antibiotic duration without increasing mortality (SAPS even suggested lower mortality). It may be raised in trauma, surgery and burns, and must not delay therapy in suspected sepsis."},{"n":72,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"A 68-year-old has suspected bacterial meningitis. Which empirical regimen is appropriate?","options":["Ceftriaxone alone","Ciprofloxacin plus metronidazole","Benzylpenicillin alone","Ceftriaxone plus vancomycin, plus amoxicillin for Listeria, with dexamethasone 10 mg 6-hourly started with or before the first antibiotic dose","Gentamicin plus clindamycin"],"answer":"D","explain":"Listeria cover is needed above 50 years, in pregnancy and in immunosuppression. Dexamethasone reduces unfavourable outcome and mortality in pneumococcal meningitis (de Gans). Do not delay antibiotics for CT; scan first only if focal signs, GCS \u003c10, papilloedema or immunocompromise."},{"n":73,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"Why is clindamycin added to a β-lactam in necrotising soft-tissue infection?","options":["It inhibits bacterial protein synthesis and suppresses toxin production","It has superior tissue penetration to carbapenems","It covers MRSA better than vancomycin","It inhibits β-lactamases","It prevents Clostridioides difficile"],"answer":"A","explain":"Clindamycin acts on the ribosome, reducing toxin and virulence factor production (streptococcal and staphylococcal) and is not affected by high inoculum (Eagle effect). Urgent surgical debridement is the priority. IVIG remains of uncertain benefit."},{"n":74,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"A patient has an NDM-1 (metallo-β-lactamase)–producing Klebsiella pneumoniae bloodstream infection. Which regimen is a recommended option?","options":["Ceftazidime–avibactam plus aztreonam","Ceftazidime–avibactam alone","Meropenem–vaborbactam alone","Ertapenem alone","Ceftolozane–tazobactam"],"answer":"A","explain":"Avibactam and vaborbactam do not inhibit metallo-β-lactamases (NDM, VIM, IMP). Aztreonam is stable to MBLs, and avibactam protects it from co-produced ESBLs and AmpC enzymes. Cefiderocol is another option."},{"n":75,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"A haemodynamically stable patient has uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia from a controlled urinary source and has been afebrile for 72 hours. What antibiotic duration is non-inferior to longer courses?","options":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days","21 days"],"answer":"C","explain":"Randomised trials (Yahav et al.; BALANCE) show 7 days is non-inferior to 14 days in bloodstream infection. Longer therapy is needed for undrained foci, endocarditis, osteomyelitis and Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia (which also needs echocardiography)."},{"n":76,"system":"Infection, Sepsis and Antimicrobials","stem":"A patient has fulminant Clostridioides difficile colitis with shock and ileus. What is the recommended treatment?","options":["Oral metronidazole alone","Loperamide","IV vancomycin alone","Oral vancomycin 125 mg alone","High-dose enteral vancomycin (500 mg 6-hourly, ± rectal) plus IV metronidazole, with urgent surgical review"],"answer":"E","explain":"IV vancomycin does not reach the colonic lumen. Surgery (subtotal colectomy or diverting loop ileostomy with colonic lavage) is considered for perforation, toxic megacolon or lactate \u003e5 mmol/L. Fidaxomicin and oral vancomycin are first line for non-fulminant disease."},{"n":77,"system":"Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","stem":"A patient with DKA has K⁺ 2.9 mmol/L on admission. Which statement is correct?","options":["Start insulin immediately and replace potassium later","Give sodium bicarbonate first","Give no potassium until K⁺ \u003e5.5 mmol/L","Give phosphate routinely","Delay insulin until K⁺ \u003e3.3 mmol/L while replacing potassium"],"answer":"E","explain":"Insulin drives potassium into cells and may provoke arrhythmias or respiratory muscle weakness. Hold insulin if K⁺ \u003c3.3 mmol/L. Typical regimen: fixed-rate insulin 0.1 U/kg/h, potassium 20–30 mmol per litre once K⁺ is 3.3–5.3, and dextrose added when glucose falls below about 14 mmol/L."},{"n":78,"system":"Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","stem":"Which feature best distinguishes hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS) from DKA?","options":["Marked hyperglycaemia (\u003e30 mmol/L) with osmolality \u003e320 mOsm/kg and minimal ketosis","Metabolic acidosis with ketones \u003e3 mmol/L","Kussmaul respiration","Abdominal pain","Hypokalaemia"],"answer":"A","explain":"HHS involves profound dehydration (about 10–12 L), so fluids come first and insulin (0.05 U/kg/h) is started only once glucose stops falling by 3–5 mmol/L/h with fluids. Lower sodium and osmolality slowly. Give prophylactic LMWH because of high VTE risk."},{"n":79,"system":"Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","stem":"What did the ADRENAL trial show regarding hydrocortisone 200 mg/day infusion in septic shock?","options":["No difference in 90-day mortality, but faster shock resolution and shorter ventilation","Reduced 90-day mortality","Increased 90-day mortality","Prolonged shock duration","A higher rate of GI bleeding"],"answer":"A","explain":"ADRENAL showed no mortality difference but faster shock reversal and fewer transfusions. APROCCHSS (hydrocortisone plus fludrocortisone) reduced mortality. SSC 2021 suggests hydrocortisone 200 mg/day when norepinephrine is ≥0.25 µg/kg/min for ≥4 hours."},{"n":80,"system":"Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","stem":"In thyroid storm, why should iodine be given at least one hour after a thionamide?","options":["To prevent anaphylaxis","Because thionamides increase iodine uptake","To avoid hypothyroidism","To prevent iodine acting as substrate for new hormone synthesis before synthesis is blocked","Because iodine inactivates propylthiouracil"],"answer":"D","explain":"Thionamides block hormone synthesis; iodine (Wolff–Chaikoff effect) then blocks hormone release. Propylthiouracil and propranolol also inhibit peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion. Add hydrocortisone and treat the precipitant."},{"n":81,"system":"Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","stem":"A hypothermic, bradycardic, hyponatraemic patient is suspected of myxoedema coma. Which management is appropriate?","options":["Rapid active external rewarming","Hypotonic fluids for the hyponatraemia","Thyroid hormone only","Sedatives for agitation","IV levothyroxine loading with IV hydrocortisone, given before or together with the thyroid hormone"],"answer":"E","explain":"Concomitant adrenal insufficiency may be unmasked by thyroid hormone, so give hydrocortisone. Rewarm passively, as active rewarming causes vasodilatation and collapse. Use lower levothyroxine doses in the elderly or cardiac patients; manage hypoventilation and fluid-restrict for hyponatraemia."},{"n":82,"system":"Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders","stem":"What blood glucose target is recommended for critically ill adults?","options":["4.4–6.1 mmol/L (80–110 mg/dL)","6.0–8.0 mmol/L","10–14 mmol/L","Treat only if glucose \u003e20 mmol/L","7.8–10 mmol/L (140–180 mg/dL)"],"answer":"E","explain":"NICE-SUGAR found intensive control (4.5–6.0 mmol/L) increased 90-day mortality (27.5% vs 24.9%) and severe hypoglycaemia. Start insulin at \u003e10 mmol/L and aim for 7.8–10 mmol/L."},{"n":83,"system":"Toxicology","stem":"Which statement about the Rumack–Matthew nomogram is correct?","options":["It applies to a single acute ingestion with a serum level taken at least 4 hours after ingestion","It is valid for staggered overdoses","A level taken 1 hour after ingestion can be plotted","It is valid for chronic therapeutic excess","It applies only to patients who have vomited"],"answer":"A","explain":"For staggered or uncertain-time ingestions, or levels before 4 hours, treat with N-acetylcysteine based on risk and dose. Acetylcysteine is most effective within 8 hours but still used later and in established liver injury."},{"n":84,"system":"Toxicology","stem":"A patient with unexplained high anion gap metabolic acidosis, osmolar gap 40 mOsm/kg, AKI and calcium oxalate crystals in the urine ingested ethylene glycol. What is the most appropriate treatment?","options":["Activated charcoal 50 g","N-acetylcysteine","Fomepizole 15 mg/kg IV, with haemodialysis for severe acidosis or renal injury","Flumazenil","Sodium bicarbonate alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Fomepizole inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing formation of glycolic and oxalic acid. Dialysis removes the parent alcohol and metabolites in severe poisoning (pH ≤7.3, AKI, high level). Methanol poisoning is similar (visual loss, formate; give folinic acid)."},{"n":85,"system":"Toxicology","stem":"Which management pitfall must be avoided in severe salicylate poisoning?","options":["IV sodium bicarbonate","Activated charcoal","Haemodialysis","Intubation and ventilation without matching the patient's high minute ventilation","Dextrose supplementation"],"answer":"D","explain":"Falling minute ventilation after intubation causes acidaemia, increasing non-ionised salicylate entry into the brain. Alkalinise with IV bicarbonate (urine pH 7.5–8.5) and give dextrose even if glucose is normal. Dialyse for level \u003e7.2 mmol/L, altered mental state, AKI, pulmonary oedema or refractory acidosis."},{"n":86,"system":"Toxicology","stem":"Why is conventional pulse oximetry unreliable in carbon monoxide poisoning?","options":["It cannot distinguish carboxyhaemoglobin from oxyhaemoglobin and overestimates saturation","Carboxyhaemoglobin absorbs red light weakly, giving falsely low readings","It detects only deoxyhaemoglobin","CO converts haemoglobin to methaemoglobin","CO shifts the saturation curve to the right"],"answer":"A","explain":"Use arterial co-oximetry to measure COHb. Treat with 100% oxygen; hyperbaric oxygen is considered for loss of consciousness, neurological deficit, ischaemic ECG changes, pregnancy or high COHb levels. Delayed neurological sequelae may follow."},{"n":87,"system":"Toxicology","stem":"A patient with amitriptyline overdose has QRS 140 ms, hypotension and a seizure. What is the most appropriate therapy?","options":["IV phenytoin","IV flecainide","IV physostigmine","IV flumazenil","IV sodium bicarbonate 50–100 mmol bolus, targeting arterial pH 7.45–7.55 and QRS narrowing"],"answer":"E","explain":"Tricyclic toxicity involves sodium channel blockade; sodium loading and alkalinisation narrow the QRS and improve hypotension. Treat seizures with benzodiazepines. Avoid class I antiarrhythmics, physostigmine and flumazenil, which may precipitate seizures or asystole. Lipid emulsion is a rescue option."},{"n":88,"system":"Trauma, Burns, Obstetric and Environmental Emergencies","stem":"A 70 kg adult has 40% TBSA burns sustained at 14:00. Using the Parkland formula (4 mL/kg/%TBSA), what infusion rate is required in the first 8 hours?","options":["350 mL/h","500 mL/h","700 mL/h","1,400 mL/h","2,800 mL/h"],"answer":"C","explain":"Total = 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL over 24 hours, with half (5,600 mL) in the first 8 hours from time of injury: 700 mL/h. Titrate to urine output 0.5 mL/kg/h to avoid 'fluid creep'; use Ringer's lactate."},{"n":89,"system":"Trauma, Burns, Obstetric and Environmental Emergencies","stem":"During magnesium sulfate infusion for severe pre-eclampsia, which is the earliest clinical sign of magnesium toxicity?","options":["Hypertension","Loss of patellar (deep tendon) reflexes","Tachycardia","Fever","Pupillary dilatation"],"answer":"B","explain":"After a 4 g load and 1 g/h infusion (Magpie), reflex loss occurs at about 3.5–5 mmol/L, then respiratory depression and finally cardiac arrest. Magnesium is renally excreted, so monitor urine output. Antidote: calcium gluconate 1 g IV."},{"n":90,"system":"Trauma, Burns, Obstetric and Environmental Emergencies","stem":"In which trauma patient is permissive hypotension contraindicated?","options":["Penetrating torso trauma with ongoing haemorrhage","A patient with severe traumatic brain injury","Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm","Pelvic fracture with haemorrhage","Blunt abdominal trauma with bleeding"],"answer":"B","explain":"In uncontrolled haemorrhage, accept SBP 80–90 mmHg until surgical control, but in TBI or spinal injury adequate cerebral/spinal perfusion needs SBP ≥100–110 mmHg. Limit the duration of permissive hypotension."},{"n":91,"system":"Trauma, Burns, Obstetric and Environmental Emergencies","stem":"A patient found in snow with a core temperature of 22 °C is in ventricular fibrillation arrest. Which approach is best?","options":["Declare death after 20 minutes of CPR","Continue CPR and rewarm with ECLS/ECMO if available, limiting shocks and withholding drugs until core temperature \u003e30 °C","Give adrenaline 1 mg every 3–5 minutes","Stop CPR if no ROSC after three shocks","Passive rewarming only"],"answer":"B","explain":"'Nobody is dead until warm and dead' (≥32 °C). ERC guidance: up to three shocks and no drugs while below 30 °C. ECLS rewarming gives the best survival; serum K⁺ \u003e12 mmol/L suggests a non-survivable asphyxial arrest."},{"n":92,"system":"Trauma, Burns, Obstetric and Environmental Emergencies","stem":"An adult develops anaphylactic shock after an IV antibiotic. What is the first-line drug, dose and route?","options":["Adrenaline 1 mg IV bolus","Chlorphenamine IV","Hydrocortisone 200 mg IV","Adrenaline 0.5 mg (500 µg) intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh","Nebulised salbutamol"],"answer":"D","explain":"IM adrenaline is first line, repeated every 5 minutes if needed. Use an IV adrenaline infusion (experienced clinician) for refractory shock. Give IV fluid 500–1000 mL. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts. Measure mast cell tryptase at 1–2 hours."},{"n":93,"system":"Critical Care Pharmacology and Sedation","stem":"Which combination is characteristic of propofol infusion syndrome?","options":["Hypokalaemia, alkalosis and ileus","Hyperglycaemia, hypothermia and bradycardia","Hypertension, mydriasis and fever","Metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia and cardiac failure","Hyponatraemia and SIADH"],"answer":"D","explain":"PRIS follows high doses (\u003e4 mg/kg/h) for \u003e48 hours, especially with catecholamines or steroids, through impaired mitochondrial fatty-acid oxidation. Stop propofol and give supportive care (haemofiltration, ECMO in refractory cases)."},{"n":94,"system":"Critical Care Pharmacology and Sedation","stem":"In the ROSE trial of moderate–severe ARDS, early continuous neuromuscular blockade with deep sedation, compared with light sedation, resulted in:","options":["Reduced 90-day mortality","Increased ventilator-free days","No improvement in 90-day in-hospital mortality","Reduced barotrauma","Reduced ICU-acquired weakness"],"answer":"C","explain":"ROSE (mortality 42.5% vs 42.8%) contradicted the earlier ACURASYS signal. Neuromuscular blockade is now reserved for dyssynchrony, high plateau pressures or during proning, with train-of-four monitoring."},{"n":95,"system":"Critical Care Pharmacology and Sedation","stem":"Why does remifentanil have a context-sensitive half-time independent of infusion duration?","options":["Hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism","It is hydrolysed by non-specific plasma and tissue esterases","Unchanged renal excretion","Redistribution to fat","Hofmann degradation"],"answer":"B","explain":"Rapid esterase hydrolysis gives a context-sensitive half-time of about 3–5 minutes, whereas fentanyl's rises substantially with prolonged infusions due to redistribution and accumulation. Hofmann degradation is the pathway for atracurium and cisatracurium."},{"n":96,"system":"Critical Care Pharmacology and Sedation","stem":"Which pharmacodynamic index best predicts the efficacy of β-lactam antibiotics?","options":["Peak concentration to MIC ratio","Volume of distribution","The time that free drug concentration exceeds the MIC (fT\u003eMIC)","Post-antibiotic effect duration alone","Renal clearance"],"answer":"C","explain":"β-lactams are time-dependent, so extended or continuous infusion maximises fT\u003eMIC; BLING III reported lower 90-day mortality with continuous infusion. Aminoglycosides are concentration-dependent (Cmax/MIC), and vancomycin is guided by AUC/MIC."},{"n":97,"system":"Statistics, Ethics and Quality","stem":"In a randomised trial, 28-day mortality was 30% in controls and 24% in the intervention arm. What is the number needed to treat to prevent one death?","options":["6","10","17","25","50"],"answer":"C","explain":"ARR = 30% − 24% = 6%. NNT = 1/0.06 ≈ 17. The relative risk is 0.8 and the relative risk reduction 20%."},{"n":98,"system":"Statistics, Ethics and Quality","stem":"A test has sensitivity 90% and specificity 80%. In a population with 10% prevalence, what is the positive predictive value?","options":["20%","33%","50%","80%","90%"],"answer":"B","explain":"In 1,000 patients: 100 diseased (90 true positives, 10 false negatives) and 900 non-diseased (720 true negatives, 180 false positives). PPV = 90/(90 + 180) = 33%; NPV = 720/730 = 98.6%. PPV rises with prevalence."},{"n":99,"system":"Statistics, Ethics and Quality","stem":"A family insists on full escalation for a patient with irreversible multiorgan failure, while the ICU team considers further treatment non-beneficial. What is the best approach?","options":["Withdraw treatment unilaterally without discussion","Structured communication and goals-of-care discussion, with a time-limited trial, second opinion and palliative or ethics involvement if needed","Continue all treatments on request indefinitely","Transfer the patient to avoid conflict","Defer entirely to the most senior relative regardless of the patient's wishes"],"answer":"B","explain":"Shared decision-making informed by the patient's values (substituted judgement) is key. Withholding and withdrawing treatment are ethically equivalent. Legal frameworks differ between countries; document discussions clearly."},{"n":100,"system":"Statistics, Ethics and Quality","stem":"Which of the following was NOT part of the evidence-based central-line insertion bundle that reduced CLABSI in the Keystone ICU project?","options":["Routine replacement of catheters over a guidewire every 7 days","Hand hygiene","Maximal sterile barrier precautions","Chlorhexidine skin antisepsis","Avoiding the femoral site and daily review of line necessity"],"answer":"A","explain":"Pronovost et al. reported a sustained reduction (about 66%) in catheter-related bloodstream infection from a bundle of hand hygiene, maximal barrier precautions, chlorhexidine skin preparation, avoiding the femoral site and removing unnecessary lines. Routine catheter exchange increases infection risk."}]};
